{"name":"Tennis Blue","hex":"#7cb5c6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tennis","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sport played by two players (or four in doubles), who alternately strike the ball over a net using racquets.","A match in this sport."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To play tennis.","To drive backward and forward like a tennis ball."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tennis"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tennis","blocks":[{"text":"Tennis is a racket sport that is played either individually against a single opponent (singles) or between two teams of two players each (doubles). Each player uses a tennis racket strung with a cord to strike a hollow rubber ball covered with felt over or around a net and into the opponent's court. The objective is to manoeuvre the ball in such a way that the opponent is not able to play a valid return. If a player is unable to return the ball successfully, the opponent scores a point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/2013_Australian_Open_-_Guillaume_Rufin.jpg/330px-2013_Australian_Open_-_Guillaume_Rufin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennis"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/EcoleCremonaiseTennis.jpg/500px-EcoleCremonaiseTennis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/EcoleCremonaiseTennis.jpg/500px-EcoleCremonaiseTennis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Painting from Cremona in the late-16th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEcoleCremonaiseTennis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Jeu_de_paume002.jpg/500px-Jeu_de_paume002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Jeu_de_paume002.jpg/500px-Jeu_de_paume002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jeu de paume in the 17th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJeu_de_paume002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Tennis_birthplace_Edgbaston.jpg/500px-Tennis_birthplace_Edgbaston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Tennis_birthplace_Edgbaston.jpg/500px-Tennis_birthplace_Edgbaston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u